        "id": "what-is-lipo-c",
        "level": 2,
        "heading": "What Is Lipo-C?",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Lipo-C is a name used for multi-component lipotropic research formulations; its exact composition is formulation-specific.",
            "label": "Short answer"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Search for Lipo-C and pharmacy pages, clinic pages, supplier listings and forum threads all use the same name. However, they are not always describing the same thing. Lipo-C is not a single molecule with a fixed structure, but a name applied to blends of several compounds. There is no shared standard behind the name, so each listing describes its own blend. That makes the name useful as a search term and unreliable as a specification."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "“Lipotropic” is the word usually attached to these blends. In the literature it refers to substances involved in lipid transport and metabolism, especially in the liver (NCBI MeSH). That describes a category of biological function, not a product recipe, so it says little about any one blend."
          },
          {
            "type": "h3",
            "text": "Why the name alone is not enough"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "In practice, this means the name by itself does not fully identify the material. Two products called Lipo-C can differ in the ingredients they contain and the chemical forms used. They can also differ in how much of each is present and how the material is supplied. A researcher who records only “Lipo-C” in a lab notebook has recorded very little."
          },
          {
            "type": "h3",
            "text": "Two identities to keep apart"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Two kinds of identity therefore need to stay separate. The identity of the whole blend comes from a product specification and lot documentation. The identity of each ingredient comes from the published literature on that compound. Each answers a different question, and neither can stand in for the other."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "This matters most for reproducibility, because results cannot be traced back to a defined material when the composition is unclear. The sections below cover what to verify, how to read the evidence and what a certificate of analysis should show. Related names such as “Lipo-C with B12” and “MIC-B12” appear in the same listings and are covered further down."
          }
