        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Research relevant to Lipo-C can be read at three layers, but they are not interchangeable."
          },
          {
            "type": "h3",
            "text": "Layer 1 — Formulation evidence"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Research performed on the actual combination. This is therefore the only layer that can speak directly to a blend. No direct formulation-level study was identified in the literature reviewed. Because the current Veracue composition is not publicly specified, published ingredient-level studies cannot be treated as direct evidence for a specific Veracue formulation. That reflects the limits of the literature reviewed, not proof that no such study could exist."
          },
          {
            "type": "h3",
            "text": "Layer 2 — Ingredient evidence"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Research on individual components in cells, animals or people. Each study describes one compound, in one form, in one setting, so it informs how that component is understood and nothing more."
          },
          {
            "type": "h3",
            "text": "Layer 3 — Mechanistic evidence"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Biochemical, cellular or animal work that explains why a compound might matter, such as early animal studies of choline and liver fat (Best and Ridout, 1933). A rationale explains why something might matter, but it does not show an outcome."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Evidence from an individual ingredient does not automatically become evidence for the complete Lipo-C formulation."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "L-carnitine illustrates why ingredient-level evidence must be kept separate from formulation-level evidence. Where a Lipo-C formulation contains L-carnitine, literature on L-carnitine can inform interpretation of that component, but it does not establish efficacy of the complete blend."
          }
        ]
      },
