              {
                "number": "01",
                "question": "What exactly does “Lipo-C” identify?",
                "problem": "A supplier page, a clinic page and a forum thread can all say “Lipo-C” and mean different products, so recording that name alone leaves a notebook entry that cannot be traced to a defined material.",
                "solution": "The name works as a category label, not a complete identity. A complete identity combines the name with a product specification (ingredients, chemical forms, concentrations, volume and physical form) and the lot it applies to. Until those details are confirmed, treat “Lipo-C” as shorthand for a type of formulation rather than a defined substance."
              },
              {
                "number": "02",
                "question": "Is Lipo-C a peptide?",
                "problem": "Lipo-C is often listed beside peptide products, which blurs the line between a product category and a chemical class.",
                "solution": "Chemical classification depends on composition. A peptide is a defined amino-acid chain, while a multi-component blend is a mixture of separate compounds with no single sequence. Peptide-specific descriptors, such as sequence identity or one peptide molecular weight, therefore do not apply to a blend unless documentation for a specific lot shows otherwise. Ask what the material contains, not what it is listed next to."
              },
              {
                "number": "03",
                "question": "How do I verify the actual composition?",
                "problem": "A product name and a short description rarely list every ingredient, and pages can lag behind the material that is actually supplied.",
                "solution": "Request the product specification and the documentation for your lot. Then confirm each ingredient by name and chemical form, its concentration with units, the total volume or amount, and any additional ingredients such as diluents. Compare those details with the lot documentation, and note any field the documentation does not cover. The specification, not the listing title, is the reference."
              },
              {
                "number": "04",
                "question": "Can I use another supplier's Lipo-C formula?",
                "problem": "Another supplier's Lipo-C formula is easy to find and can look like a standard.",
                "solution": "No. Supplier formulations are not interchangeable specifications. Another company's ingredient list, concentrations or certificate describes that company's product only, and it says nothing about a different supplier's material. If a list looks authoritative, check which product and lot it actually belongs to. Public listings show, at most, that the name varies. They should not be copied into a study record as if they described the material you hold."
              },
              {
                "number": "05",
                "question": "How should I read ingredient-level studies?",
                "problem": "Studies exist for compounds that appear in various Lipo-C listings, and it is tempting to read them as evidence for a blend.",
                "solution": "Match four things: the exact component, its chemical form, the model (cells, animals or people) and the outcome measured. A trial of one compound in one population answers a question about that compound in that population. It becomes relevant to a specific formulation only when the documented composition includes the component, and even then it stays ingredient-level evidence."
              },
              {
                "number": "06",
                "question": "Does ingredient evidence prove the full blend?",
                "problem": "Marketing often moves from “each ingredient has a role” to “the blend works.”",
                "solution": "No. Combination evidence has to be evaluated on its own, because a blend can behave differently from its parts. Effects, interactions and any additive or synergistic benefit therefore cannot be assumed from single-compound studies. Claims about a combination need studies that actually tested the combination, in a defined formulation, with outcomes measured. Until such a study exists, the honest position is that the question remains open."
              },
              {
                "number": "07",
                "question": "What should I look for on a COA?",
                "problem": "A certificate can look reassuring while saying little about the material you hold.",
                "solution": "Read it for what it documents: the product name, lot number, tested components, analytical method, measured result, test date and laboratory. Also check that the lot matches your material and that each ingredient you care about was actually tested. Compare the specification with the result rather than looking only for a pass mark. An ingredient that does not appear on the certificate has not been confirmed by it."
              },
              {
                "number": "08",
                "question": "Why do Lipo-C products have different formulas?",
                "problem": "Two products with the same name can list different ingredients, forms and strengths.",
                "solution": "Lipo-C is not a universal formulation standard, so each supplier defines its own blend. Because no shared specification fixes the components or how they are supplied, B12-containing versions can also differ in the B12 form. The variation reflects how the name is used in the market rather than a scientific definition. That is why the specification for each product is the only dependable reference."
              },
              {
                "number": "09",
                "question": "Why do people search “Lipo-C peptide”?",
                "problem": "“Lipo-C peptide” is one of the most frequent searches for this material, which can suggest it belongs in the peptide category.",
                "solution": "Search behavior follows context. Lipo-C is sold and discussed alongside peptides, so people combine the words when they look for it. Query volume shows what people type, but it does not classify a substance. Scientific identity comes from composition. Using the phrase to find this page is fine. Treating it as chemistry is where the confusion starts."
              },
              {
                "number": "10",
                "question": "How should I evaluate metabolic or fat-loss claims?",
                "problem": "Fat-loss language around Lipo-C is common and rarely shows what kind of evidence stands behind it.",
                "solution": "Sort each claim by evidence type. Is it a biological rationale, an ingredient-level study, an animal finding or a direct human study of the combination? A rationale explains why something might matter, while an outcome has to be measured. Where only rationale or single-ingredient data exist, the claim is unverified for the formulation, whatever the volume of marketing around it."
              },
              {
                "number": "11",
                "question": "What should be recorded for reproducible research?",
                "problem": "Gaps in material records surface later, when a result has to be reproduced or explained.",
                "solution": "Record the product name, specification version, lot number and receipt date. Then add the ingredient list with forms and concentrations, physical form, COA reference and any storage information taken from the applicable documentation. Where a field cannot be filled from documentation, mark it as unconfirmed rather than assuming. The study record then shows exactly what was and was not known when the work was done."
